A||||18||Ozzy||Questions on '87 GT...||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||12-09-1999||12:02 AM||||Hey Gang. <P>Recently I've been having a series of 'pain in the butt' problems with my '88 Coupe. I'm gonna get to the bottom of them eventually but...I'm thinking of unloading it and picking up a GT. I prefer the look of the GT anyway. There are quite a few '88 & '87 GT's running around here in the Northwest but the "bargains" ya'wl seem to report in the east I am yet to find here. However, I digress! The '88 GT's around here are a little more than I wan't to pay for a Fiero.<P>My questions concerning '87 GT's are these: <P>Is the suspension and steering upgraded on this car. (As in the same suspension on my '88 Coupe)<P>Did they come stock with the 15" rims?<P>Do they have the same steering rack as my coupe?<P>Was there a 5 speed available?<P>Can I find one that NEVER breaks down??? (kidding)<P>Thnx for any info.<P>Ozzy||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-09-1999||12:42 AM||||The suspension, etc on your '88 was the new and improved one.  The susp, etc on an '87 was the same one used on all pre-'88s.  The '87 GTs did have 15" rims as stock.  Two different widths, though.  (7"/front and 8"/rear, I believe)  I think there were 2 different 5-spds available.  An Isuzu and the Getrag.  Definitely go for the Getrag!  And, yes.  You can find one that never breaks down.  It's called the brand new, never titled Fiero that sits in you garage under the cover and never gets driven!  Personally, I would either convert yours to a GT or save the money to get a real '88 GT.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 12-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||12-09-1999||01:38 AM||||The Getrag 5 spds were on all the V6's from 86.5 'till the end, unless the owner swapped his 4banger out.  The Isuzu's were used on the 4's, said they couldn't handle the torque of the V6 but most of what I've read says that they hold up fine..||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||12-09-1999||07:03 AM||||I have an 86.5 GT that has the 4 speed? I know the car is original. Was the 5 speed Getrag available before 87? If so it was an option. I don,t believe the 4 speed was availible in the GT in 87 or 88.Anyone know for certain about this.||||reg||5
Z||000004||fiero56||12-09-1999||07:52 AM||||The v-6 5-speed getrags are sorta rare.  I belive they came out sometime around mid summer.  If you have a 4-speed yours is from the earlier production of GT's before the 5-speed was available.  Also the '86-'87 GT's all had 15x7 wheels.  Tires were 205/60/15 in front, and 215/60/15 in the rear. '88 is the only model where the GT had different wheel widths front and back.<P>The 4 speed for the v-6 was only available for '85 and part of '86.<BR>Another tid-bit of information is, the 4-speed used on the v-6 was the same 4-speed used on the '84 4-cyl.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||12-09-1999||08:13 AM||||Fiero56, The 86.5 GT has the fast back, doesn't that make it from the later part of the year.<BR> The Wheels on my 87GT are all the same width.  I thought that was only the 88. <BR> I believe the 87 only had different size tires front and rear.<BR> Was the final drive the same on the V6 4 speed.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000006||fiero56||12-09-1999||08:25 AM||||Acutally all '86 GTs are fastbacks. The .5 just means the model didn't come out until half way through the production year. Sort of a sales gimmick. I believe Ford did the same .5 with the mustang one year. <BR>My motorweek video and Fiero Spotters Guide both say '86 and '87 had the same size wheels, but tire sizes were different(see above). <P>There were two final drives in '84. One was the economy with 3.32 fd. The other was the performance with 4.10 fd. The '86 v-6 4-speed had a final drive of 3.65.<BR>I have Fiero transmission gearing on my site at: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Pit/3490/html/specs.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Pit/3490/html/specs.html</A> <P>If anyone claims to have an '86GT notchback, I would like to know what the VIN is. <BR>To be an '86GT notchback it would start out: 1G2PG379GP******.<BR>To be an '86GT fastback you would replace the "37" with a "97"<BR>(The asteriks would be production number out of 83,974)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wolfhound||12-09-1999||09:53 AM||||I had thought only the 85s GT were notchback, but then read some where on the form that the 86.5 was the first fastback. I had never heard of an 86.5 until I found this forum.<BR>Good info on your site 56!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-09-1999||07:09 PM||||All the GTs built in the 1st half of the '86 production year were notchbacks.  There's a guy my uncle knows with an '86 GT notchback.  They changed to the fastback style mid year.  I don't know how many were produced, but probably not alot.  I would imagine that there were less GTs built than the rest of the models and 1/2 a year isn't much considering an automotive production year really only lasts about 10 months.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||12-10-1999||07:40 AM||||I checked with Ed Parks on this last night, and here is what he told me in an e-mail.<P>"Drew, no notchbacks built that have ever been verified. The reason for<BR>the late introduction of the GT in 86 was the qtr windows. A real B***h<BR>to make. Fastback was designed in 83."<P>I am on several Fiero lists, this topic has been brought up several times, and the the resulting answer has been that there were no notchback GTs made in '86.  If this guys car is really an '86 GT, then I would like to know what his VIN is. Sounds more likely to be '86 or '87 SE. Both of those models are the aero notchback.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||01:33 PM||||I'll check with the guy next time I see him although he probably won't want to give away his VIN.  Is there any other way to check?||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||12-10-1999||02:35 PM||||The fun part about the transition to the GT was explained real well in the Witzenberg Fiero book. In 85 the GT notch back was born. In 86 the GT disappeared and the old GT notchback was turned into the SE. The only thing they changed was the quarter panel windows to SE from GT. The early GT fastbacks came onto the seen but at with a 4 speed because of unspecified problems with the Getrag/Muncie availability. From midyear 86 the Fiero (much to the praise of car mags.) had a 5 speed. <P>5 speed Getrag/Muncies Fieros rare? Not really. At least in So California, they are everywhere. <P>Years and years ago we built up a 3.2 V6 for a guy named Bill Prince for his rebody. It was an 87 4 cyl with the Isuzu tranny and he wanted to keep it. So we built it for him and aside from having to build a custom air intake to clear the shifting cables we have never heard him have any problems. He sat in the garage and actually told us how he was going to try to abuse it and break it! "If if doesn't break something than Im not pushing hard enough!" You can imagine the look on our faces!<P>The 4 speed tranny with 4.10s on the 84 is NOT the same on the V6. We had a customer find that out the hard way. He had a tranny replaced and they put on an 84 tranny with the 4.10s. The case cracked. We pulled out a V6 4 speed tranny and found that in a few places the case had been reenforced. The intereseting thing is that the difference between a 4.10 cog and a 3.65 (If I remember correctly) is 3 more teeth on the gear! As an experiment we put the 4.10 gear in a V6 tranny case and it has been running great ever since and its hooked up to a tricked out 3.4 V6 now. (CRAPPY gas mileage though especially on the freeway!)<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||four50four||12-10-1999||04:42 PM||||i think what he meant when he said 5speed getrags were rare, was that they were rare in 86, or 86 1/2...||||reg||1
Z||000013||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||08:12 PM||||I know that I am a little late to chime in... but the network has been down for the last two days ;p <P>Monkeyman,<P>I hate to disagree with you, but fiero56 is right.  There is no such thing as the 1986 Notchback GT.  Although I think that there are more believers in it than in Bigfoot (Not the Monster Truck).  Furthermore, someone shouldn't be weary of giving someone else their VIN.  Unless over there in IN they have figured out some crazy way to break into your personal information by the car you drive.  Anyway, they are in no way confidential since you can look right through the windshield and see them.  Their main purpose is to be seen and therefore increase the difficulty of stealing a vehicle.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||08:15 PM||||Oh yeah... If you have read my Subwoofer post... I hate to be a prick again... but the 86.5 thing is pretty silly to many of us.  All 1986 GT's are 1986 GT's.  If you feel the need to be specific, do so by stating it is a 4-spd/5-spd since that is the ONLY difference.  If it is an auto... well you really have no excuse.<P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>Proud owner of : 1987.9375(15/16ths) SC and 1985.046875 (3/64ths) GT<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ozzy||12-10-1999||08:28 PM||||Wow... You guys are fulla information. Most of it is not too relevant to me right now but I need to definatley identify the difference between the notch back and fast back 'cause I do know which one I like the look of. Apart from that, I just decided I need to look for an'88 GT 'cause I dont want the older suspension style. It will be hard enough familiarizing myself with a completely different engine without worrying about the rest of the car.<P>BTW anyone want a cute lil' '88 Coupe??<BR>(yeah, I know...wrong place to post!)<P>Ozzy O. Australian, I.||||reg||7
Z||000016||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||09:17 PM||||My bad, guys!  I made a call today about the '86 is-it-a-GT-or-not.  I got the VIN (no prob there-guess I'm just paranoid!), checked it out and-guess what-it's an SE.  Sorry.  We'll try again later when we actually know what we're talking about. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||09:29 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>No problem... we are just trying our best to make sure you have as Paul Harvey would say "the rest of the story."<P>Ozzy,<P>How true.  It is amazing how far off these threads sometimes get.  Anyway, the 1987 & 1988 Coupes DO NOT have the same suspension as the WS6 equippt GT's and Formulas.  It is essentially identical to the pre-87's.  Those lighting their flame throwers, consult your 22P first.  I acknowledge that changes were made.  But not significant ones.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||12-10-1999||10:14 PM||||There is a way to give your VIN without giving away your <B> exact </B> VIN. With the Fiero just leave off the last six numbers and people will have no idea what car you have. The last six numbers tell what production number your vehicle is.  The earlier numbers tell about body, where it was made, engine, year, etc. Just another little tid-bit.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>William, question for you. I've never seen a pre '88 suspension so I'm just inquiring here. I believe the '84-'87 front suspension had the shocks offset from the springs.  Was this also true for the '88 coupe?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||14
